  "explanation": "All 29 candidate patches are pure condition-gate edits that gate existing blocks with additional conjunctive terms. They do not target the root cause of the observed failure: o_sck is stuck high (1) during the STARTUP_CLOCKS window (cycles 26-100) when the reference expects it to toggle at the divided SPI clock rate. The public trace shows 1,106,750 cycle mismatches beginning at cycle 94 on o_sck, and the startup hold releases exactly at cycle 101. The original llsdspi startup block (WAIT_FOR_STARTUP) only decrements startup_counter on rising edges of o_sclk, but o_sclk itself is held high by the 'if (startup_hold) o_sclk <= !o_sclk;' clause that executes only when r_z_counter asserts. With the default INITIAL_CLKDIV=0x63 (99), r_z_counter stays low for 99 cycles after reset, so no rising edge occurs and startup_counter never decrements — the design then relies on the startup_hold timeout to expire, but it never does. None of the candidate patches modify the startup block's edge detection, the r_z_counter initialization, the clock-divider load timing, or the hold-release logic. Therefore no candidate can close the observed gap, and ranking them would imply a false prospect of correction."
}
